  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in California totaled $47.1B in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 203,183 awards with an average award size of $232K. Obligations fell 24.0% year over year. Annual obligations peaked at $62B in FY25 and declined to $47.1B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state, obligating $25.8B, followed by the Department of Energy at $6.3B and the Department of Homeland Security at $4.9B. Research and development work leads industry spending with two R&D categories accounting for $6.4B and $5.3B respectively. Major vendors include national labs, shipbuilders, and aerospace firms, with LAWRENCE LIVERMORE NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC receiving $3.4B and NATIONAL STEEL AND SHIPBUILDING COMPANY receiving $2.8B. The current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing. This snapshot highlights where federal money is concentrated across agencies, industries, and top contractors in California.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has California federal contracting changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 24.0% year over year. Annual obligations rose from $52.1B in FY21 to $62B in FY25, then declined to $47.1B in FY26. The state recorded 203,183 awards in FY2026 to date with an average award size of $232K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate federal spending in California?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $25.8B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Energy obligated $6.3B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $4.9B. Research and development is the largest industry grouping, with related categories totaling $6.4B and $5.3B, and construction and shipbuilding also appearing among the top industries."
      }
    ]
